A||||5||PaUL||Parking Brake Recall
Z||000000||PaUL||04-09-99||05:51 PM||||Just to let everyone know, GM has a parking brake recall on the Fiero. My parking brake never worked from the day I bought my car. About 3 years ago, I learned of the recall on another forum. I called the local GM dealership here in Hawaii, and lo and behold they fixed the problem with no charge! I couldnt believe it, my car was already 10 years old. So if any of you have a malfunctioning parking brake, call the your local GM dealership and get free brake work.
Z||000001||Patrick||04-09-99||11:38 PM||||Hi Paul,<p>It may not be quite that easy. I tried the same thing when I bought my Fiero two years ago. The Pontiac dealership I went to ran my VIN through the computer and told me that there were no more outstanding recalls for my Fiero. According to them, once a repair has been done, parking brake or otherwise, that's it. They also stated that in any case, there would be nothing more done for a ten year old (at that time) car.  If anyone knows for sure that I was being screwed around, please let me know.<p>And by the way, the Raybestos parking brake cable that I bought and installed two years ago is already starting to seize up.:(<p>-Patrick
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||04-10-99||12:35 AM||||What`s up Paul,<p>You can call the Auto Saftey Hotline (888)327-4236 for any info, but  Patrick is right the VIN # tells if the repairs (any recalls) have been done on your car. The dealer gets paid from GM so there should be no problems with them doing the repair. <br>The NHTSA Campaingn ID Number for the parking brake is 91V055000. Try another dealer??
Z||000003||PaUL||04-10-99||12:20 PM||||yeah, they ran my vin through the computer and wanted to perform 2 more recalls, I think they wanted to put in some kind of fire proof shielding. I told them I didnt want the other recalls, but they kept telling me I had to get them. Finally after I firmly insisted, they agreed to do just the parking brake.
Z||000004||theogre||04-10-99||08:34 PM||||Paul, the dealler pushed the other recalls for good reaasons. Yes they do get money out of it, but they can also be held legally liable for failing to perform them. I'm suprised they didn't make you sign some sort of waiver before working on the car.<p>The anti fire recalls are good things. Depending on what year you have, and just what recalls apply, you could even end up with a free motor.
Z||000005||Carrolles||04-21-99||12:53 PM||||When I bought my 85 SE in Birmingham, Alabama in 1997 it had 109000 miles on it.  I called 1(800) PMCARES and gave them the VIN.  They told me that the parking brake recall had not been performed and gave me an authorization number to take to the dealer.  I did so and got new rear brakes for free.  Not bad for a 12 year old car with over 100000 miles on it.  They also told me that recalls have no expiration date.  It does not matter how old the car is, they have to perform the repair.<br>Good luck<br>Carroll
